  {
    "id": "cemc-gauss8-1999-22",
    "bank": "cemc",
    "source": {
      "contest": "Gauss Contest (CEMC)",
      "paper": "Grade 8",
      "year": 1999,
      "number": 22,
      "url": "https://cemc.uwaterloo.ca/sites/default/files/documents/1999/1999Gauss8Contest.pdf"
    },
    "grade": "8",
    "points": 8,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "In the diagram, $AC = CB = 10$ m, where $AC$ and $CB$ are each the diameter of the small equal semi-circles. The diameter of the larger semi-circle is $AB$. In travelling from $A$ to $B$, it is possible to take one of two paths. One path goes along the semi-circular arc from $A$ to $B$. A second path goes along the semi-circular arcs from $A$ to $C$ and then along the semi-circular arc from $C$ to $B$. The difference in the lengths of these two paths is",
    "image": "qimages/cemc/gauss8-1999/q22.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$12\\pi$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$6\\pi$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$3\\pi$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$2\\pi$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "0"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "E",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Compute both",
        "body": "A semicircle with diameter $d$ has length $\\frac12 \\pi d$.\n\n- Big arc: $\\frac12 \\pi \\times 20 = 10\\pi$.\n- Two small arcs: $2 \\times \\frac12 \\pi \\times 10 = 10\\pi$.\n\nThe difference is $0$.\n\n**Answer: (E) 0**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Scaling",
        "body": "Halving the diameter halves the arc length, but there are two small arcs, so together they equal the big arc. The difference is $0$.\n\n**Answer: (E) 0**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
